00:10:55.108 --> 00:11:01.728
Adding a 3% slice means committing $1,500, yet that sleeve can swing 10% in a single

00:11:01.728 --> 00:11:04.108
week, far more than the equity side.

00:11:04.688 --> 00:11:11.408
Canadian listed crypto exchange traded funds, such as BTCX and ETHX, trade on the TSX

00:11:11.408 --> 00:11:16.828
with management fees around 1% and settle in Canadian dollars, so you never touch a wallet

00:11:16.828 --> 00:11:17.768
or a private key.

00:11:17.768 --> 00:11:23.948
The Ontario Securities Commission, or OSC, oversees those products and they come with custody

00:11:23.948 --> 00:11:29.768
at qualified banks, unlike offshore exchanges that have faced insolvency, and OSC rules

00:11:29.768 --> 00:11:32.228
require qualified third-party custodians.

00:11:32.868 --> 00:11:39.208
Gains inside a TFA stay tax-free, but any transfer to self-custody triggers a deemed disposition

00:11:39.208 --> 00:11:44.228
at fair market value, which is why most Canadian investors stay with the listed products and

00:11:44.228 --> 00:11:45.548
skip private key risk.

00:11:45.548 --> 00:11:51.568
Size the sleeve so that even a 50% drawdown leaves the overall portfolio inside your stated

00:11:51.568 --> 00:11:52.328
risk tolerance.

00:11:52.908 --> 00:11:58.768
Keep the range at 1-5%, so a bad week in crypto does not force a rebalance across the

00:11:58.768 --> 00:12:03.888
whole account, and rebalance only once a year to avoid churn outside registered accounts.

00:12:04.648 --> 00:12:07.548
Bitcoin and Ethereum funds do not carry the same risk.

00:12:08.008 --> 00:12:12.288
Bitcoin has shown lower correlation to traditional equities during risk off periods.

00:12:12.288 --> 00:12:17.788
The misconception is that a 40% rally means you should size crypto like an equity holding.

00:12:18.268 --> 00:12:24.788
The fix is a small OSC custodyed once a year sleeve that cannot wreck the TFSA on a 10%

00:12:24.788 --> 00:12:25.108
week.

